  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 300 PM EST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\nCurrently-Tonight... KMLB radar imagery shows dry conditions\nacross east central Florida. GOES-19 satellite imagery and local\nobservations indicate mostly sunny skies. Temperatures are\ncurrently seasonable in the upper 70s to mid 80s with comfortable\nhumidity with dew points in the mid 50s to upper 60s. Analysis\ncharts show high pressure (~1025mb) to the northwest of east \ncentral Florida over the Deep South. Dry weather is expected to\ncontinue tonight with seasonable to above normal lows in the upper\n50s to low 70s. Light and variable to calm winds combined with a\nweak radiation inversion will result in the potential for patchy\nfog along and north of I-4 tonight (mainly between 4AM-8AM) with\nvisibility reductions to 1 miles or less. Remember to slow down\nand use your low beam headlights if you encounter fog. \n\nTuesday-Wednesday... Rain chances increase slightly on Tuesday\nacross areas south of Melbourne with weak onshore flow in place\nand high pressure over the Deep South. A weak shortwave is \nexpected to traverse the Deep South on Wednesday which will \nprovide lift for scattered to numerous showers and isolated to \nwidely scattered lightning storms. The latest deterministic and \nRRFS guidance suggests that the potential exists for strong to \nmarginally severe storms Wednesday afternoon and evening. A \nsouthern branch of the polar front jet is forecast to strengthen \nover northern to central Florida during the aforementioned time \nperiod with a jet maxima of around 100kts combined with moderate \nCAPE values, conditionally to absolutely unstable mid level lapse \nrates, 500mb temperatures at around -10C to -12C, high bulk shear,\nand sufficient PWAT values. The primary hazards will be \noccasional to frequent lightning, wind gusts up to 40-55mph (less\nthan 5% chance for an isolated wind gusts to 60mph), small hail, \nand heavy rainfall (up to 1-3\" in 60 minutes). Additionally, there\nis the potential for patchy fog north of I-4 Wednesday morning. \nExpect seasonable afternoon highs in the low to mid 80s with \nseasonable to above normal lows in the upper 50s to low 70s.\n\nThursday-Sunday... Guidance has come into better agreement for\nlate week and into the weekend; deterministic models, as well as \nthe LREF-ensemble clusters indicate that a strong low will deepen\nacross the Deep South and Florida Thursday and Friday. At the\nsurface, a pre-frontal trough is forecast to move across east \ncentral Florida on Thursday with isolated showers mainly across \nthe Treasure Coast. The main cold front is then expected to move \neast-southeast across east central Florida Thursday night into \nFriday. Although currently rain chances are low for Thursday and \nFriday, the potential exists for rain chances to increase as we\nget closer to late week with the NBM recently having a low bias \nin precipitation due to the transition to the beginning of the \ndry season. Temperatures are expected to cool each day with \nslightly below normal highs on Thursday forecast. The coldest air \nof the season is expected Friday and over the weekend with \ntemperatures expected to reduce to up to 10-15F+ degrees below \nnormal Friday and through the weekend. Wind chills have the \npotential to reach near cold weather thresholds (40F degrees) \nFriday night and Saturday night mainly north of the Treasure \nCoast.",
